// ===========================================================================
// ⭐ THE CANONICAL TARGET for the ServiceRequest membership chain (#189).
//
// ⚠ It is the TARGET, not a description of what works. The model here is the
// CORRECT one and the implementation catches up to it. Never reshape a concept
// to make a lane pass.
//
// The acceptance criterion (operator, 2026-08-30):
//
//     an SR whose code IS in the value set          -> true  -> Approve
//     an SR whose code is NOT in it, nothing newer  -> false -> Deny
//     no SR at all, OR the SR carries no code       -> unanswered: pause and ask
//
// ⭐ THE WHOLE POINT: rows 2 and 3 are DIFFERENT. A request for some other
// service is a determinate NO — we looked, and it is not covered. No request at
// all is a question nobody has answered. Collapsing them is the defect this
// fixture exists to prevent.
//
// ⭐⭐ WHAT "NOT IN THE VALUE SET" MEANS IN ROW 2 — RULED (operator, 2026-09-02),
// because it was misread once and cost a planning round.
//
// "it" is the COVERED set, NOT the retrieve set. Row 2 says: *we adjudicate this
// kind of request, and this particular one is not covered.*
//
// A ServiceRequest for something this policy was never asked about — a
// colonoscopy, under a blepharoplasty policy — is NOT a missed row 2. It is not
// an instance of the concept at all, so it never reaches the predicate and the
// determination stays unestablished. Denying it would be a WRONG answer wearing
// a confident face; pausing says "this policy has nothing to say here", which is
// the truth.
//
// ⚠ THEREFORE `coded from` IS NOT AN OPTIMIZATION. It states what the concept is
// ABOUT — which requests are candidate answers to this question — and that is
// what makes `most recent this` MEANINGFUL. Drop it and every ServiceRequest in
// the record competes: a routine lab order dated later than the RFA request wins
// the recency race and denies the RFA. A retrieve scoped by nothing ranks by
// nothing.
//
// ⚠ So the two sets do DIFFERENT jobs and must both exist: `coded from` names
// the REQUESTABLE universe (what this concept is about), the predicate names the
// COVERED set (what this policy pays for).
//
// ⚠⚠ THE TWO ALTERNATIVES WERE WORKED THROUGH AND REJECTED. Recorded so they
// are not re-proposed — both failures are structural, not tunable:
//
//   (1) NO `coded from` — an unfiltered retrieve.
//       Every ServiceRequest in the record competes. A routine lab order dated
//       later than the request under adjudication wins the recency race and
//       DENIES it. A retrieve scoped by nothing ranks by nothing. This trades a
//       recoverable pause for a confident WRONG answer, which is worse in kind.
//
//   (2) ONE set doing both jobs — `coded from` and the predicate name the same
//       terminology.
//       Every record that survives a filter for set X is a member of X, so the
//       predicate can never return false. Row 2 disappears BY IDENTITY. The
//       source path still discriminates only if the sets differ; with one set the
//       chain behaves like a bare `exists` wearing two concepts.
//
// ⭐ WHY `matches` AND NOT `exists`. Both are rep-local projections invoked once
// per retrieved record, but they ask different questions, and the charter's rule
// is that what a construct READS decides how it reads absence:
//
//   `exists this`   asks IS THERE A RECORD — a RECORDS read. Absence ANSWERS it,
//                   so it is false, and it can NEVER pause. It also cannot tell
//                   a wrong-code request from no request: both retrieve nothing.
//   `matches … this` asks DOES THE RECORD SAY THIS — a DATUM read. A retrieved
//                   record with another code is a real `false`; no record at all
//                   leaves the arm contributing NOTHING, which is unestablished,
//                   which pauses.
//
// ⚠ An earlier sketch of this policy paired `coded from` with `exists this`.
// That is why it was wrong — not the `coded from`, which is required here
// (charter §3: it is decided by MODEL INFO, and ServiceRequest HAS a code-based
// retrieve). It was `exists`, which reads records and so cannot pause.
//
// ⭐ `coded from` names the REQUESTABLE set — what this concept is about — and
// the retrieve stays SCOPED by it for every projection. The wrong-code request
// reaches the concept because it IS a requestable service; it is then judged
// against the COVERED set, which is a DIFFERENT terminology. That difference is
// what makes a determinate `false` reachable, and it is why one set cannot do
// both jobs (see the rejected alternatives above).
//
// ⚠ The value set holds ONE code here. That is a small set, not an absent check
// (charter §3): membership is still evaluated, and the wrong-code row is what
// proves it.
// ===========================================================================
library "Endoluminal Ablation Coverage".

terminology "Endoluminal RFA VS":
- valueset is `http://example.org/procedures/ValueSet/endoluminal-rfa`.

// ONE question, three states. Its arms are the local `code is` (a clinician may
// answer it directly) and the ServiceRequest the projection judges. `most recent
// this` merges them, so a newer assertion overrides an older record and vice
// versa.
concept "Requested Service Is Endoluminal Radiofrequency Ablation":
- shape is Record.
- type is Observation.
- value type is boolean.
- code is `requested-service-is-endoluminal-rfa`.
- definition is most recent this.
- source representation:
  - type is ServiceRequest.
  - coded from "Endoluminal RFA VS".
  - value projection is matches this.

activity "Approve Endoluminal Ablation":
- request CPGCommunicationRequest.
- with `APPROVED`.

activity "Deny Endoluminal Ablation":
- request CPGCommunicationRequest.
- with `NOT APPROVED`.

// If coverage cannot be established from the data, the tree PAUSES and the
// questionnaire asks — that is the point of the chain.
decision "Endoluminal Ablation Coverage":
first:
- when "Requested Service Is Endoluminal Radiofrequency Ablation" then recommend activity "Approve Endoluminal Ablation".
- otherwise then recommend activity "Deny Endoluminal Ablation".
